p.src:
Marina defines it pragmatically. Deep tech is technology where the core science exists, often from university research, but the product will take a long time and substantial development to build: $\text{deep tech}
\approx
\text{core science exists}
+
\text{long development path}.$ Often it is a solution looking for a problem, not a problem already matched to a product. That is why the two paths must run in parallel. We do not know exactly where to take the technology until we learn who cares.

