p.src:
The next trap is confusing work volume with strategic work. A founder can get enormous satisfaction from checking off easy items. But the company needs the first item, not the seventeenth. Marina separates decisions: $\begin{aligned}
\text{strategic}
&=
\{\text{business},\ \text{team},\ \text{product},\ \text{pricing}\},\\
\text{non-strategic}
&=
\{\text{offices},\ \text{furniture},\ \text{software choice},\ \text{conference rooms}\}.\end{aligned}$ The strategic decisions must be remade when new information arrives: $\text{new information}
\Rightarrow
\text{remake strategic decisions}.$

