p.src:
But venture capital carries risks. Too much money can allow a company to move ahead before product-market fit is proven. Some markets cannot grow at the pace venture investors expect. Founders can lose control. High valuations raise the return bar. The more precise relation is: $\text{founder outcome}
\neq
\text{headline valuation}.$ A very profitable $\$10\text{M}$ company that the founder owns entirely may be a better founder outcome than pushing for a billion-dollar business and failing. The correct financing path depends on the business.

